.SS "Batch"
.sp
yabai \-m batch
.RS 4
Run several commands in one message, with one round trip to the daemon. The commands are read from stdin, one per line, and each line holds the words that follow \f(CRyabai \-m\fP, e.g. \f(CRwindow \-\-focus east\fP. A line that starts with \f(CRyabai \-m\fP (or \f(CRyabai \-\-message\fP) has that prefix removed.
.br
Words follow the quoting of sh: single and double quotes and backslash escapes, and \f(CR\*(Aq\*(Aq\fP is an empty argument. Blank lines and lines that start with \f(CR#\fP are skipped. A line is not run by a shell, so a line that a shell would compute is an error and nothing is sent: \f(CR$VARIABLE\fP, \f(CR$(...)\fP, backticks, \f(CR;\fP, \f(CR&\fP, \f(CR|\fP, \f(CR<\fP, \f(CR>\fP, parentheses, globs (\f(CR*\fP, \f(CR?\fP, \f(CR[\fP), a leading \f(CR~\fP, and an unquoted reserved word such as \f(CRif\fP, \f(CRthen\fP, \f(CRfi\fP, \f(CRwhile\fP, \f(CRdone\fP or \f(CR{\fP at the start of a line. An unterminated quote and a line with no command are errors too. Each error names its line, e.g. \f(CRyabai\-msg: batch: line 3: dynamic shell, nothing sent\fP.
.br
The commands run in order, each as a message of its own: a command that fails does not stop the others, and a batch cannot contain a batch. \fBconfig \-\-check\fP does not check the commands of a batch; they are checked when the batch runs.
.br
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
yabai \-m batch <<\*(AqEOF\*(Aq
config window_gap 6
config top_padding 12
window \-\-focus east
EOF
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
The output of a batch is the output of its commands in order, such as the value of a query. A failing command writes its failure with its index in front, where 1 is the first command of the batch. The index counts commands, not lines of stdin: blank and comment lines are not counted. The response fails, and \fByabai \-m\fP exits with status 1, when any command failed. A failing response is printed on stderr in full, so the output of the commands that succeeded goes to stderr too.
.br
A batch with no command, e.g. stdin with only blank lines, fails with \f(CRbatch: no commands given\fP. \f(CRyabai \-m batch\fP takes no arguments: with any, it fails and sends nothing. A batch is one message, so it is limited to 1 MiB like any message.
.RE
.SS "Subscribe"
.sp
yabai \-m subscribe [<EVENT>.\|.\|.]
.RS 4
Stream events to the client, one JSON line per event, until the daemon or the client ends the subscription. The events are the names of \fBsignal \-\-add\fP, e.g. \f(CRyabai \-m subscribe window_focused space_changed\fP; without an event, every event is streamed.
.br
The first line lists the subscribed events, in the order of the EVENT list under \fBSignal\fP, e.g. \f(CR{"subscribed":["space_changed","window_focused"]}\fP.
.br
Each later line is one event: its name as \fBevent\fP, then the environment values of the event as keys in lowercase, without the \fBYABAI_\fP prefix, e.g. \f(CR{"event":"window_focused","window_id":"42"}\fP. The keys are sorted and every value is a string. The line ends with a newline.
.br
The client prints each line as it arrives and exits with status 0 when the daemon closes the connection. An unknown event fails with the events it accepts, and the client exits with status 1, as for any failure.
.br
The events are not filtered by the \fBapp\fP and \fBtitle\fP filters of a signal; every event of the subscribed type is streamed. A subscription keeps its connection open, so it cannot be a command of a batch, and \fBconfig \-\-check\fP does not run it.
.br
A subscriber that does not read is dropped when more than 1 MiB of its events are waiting to be written, or when a write waits more than 5 seconds; the daemon then closes its connection. A closed client is noticed when the next event is written. Other subscribers and the window manager are not affected.
.RE
.SH "TREE LAYOUT"
.sp
\fItree\fP is the tiling layout of every space that does not use \fIfloat\fP. It arranges windows like i3 and sway do. Every window is a leaf in a tree of containers, and the space itself is the root container.
.br
A container lays out any number of children in one of three ways: \fIsplith\fP (side by side), \fIsplitv\fP (on top of each other) or \fItabbed\fP (one child visible at a time, with a row of tabs on top).
.br
Each container remembers which of its children was focused last.
.sp
A new window opens right after the focused window, inside the same container. While a container is selected with \fBwindow \-\-focus parent\fP, it opens right after that container instead, like i3 and sway. With \fBtree_autotiling\fP, the focused window is first split along its longer side.
.br
When the last window of a container closes, the container is removed as well.
.br
Containers that would only nest a split inside a split of the other orientation are flattened again.
.sp
Tabbed containers draw a tab bar with the \fBtab_bar_\(rs\fP* settings, also when they hold a single tab.
.br
A tab that holds a container is titled with its layout and applications, e.g. \fIH[iTerm2 Safari]\fP.
.br
The windows of hidden tabs keep the frame of the container and are ordered below the active tab. This needs the scripting addition when a tab holds more than one window.
.sp
Sizes are shares of the parent container, like i3\(cqs \fIpercent\fP. \fBwindow \-\-resize\fP moves an edge of the window, taking the space from or giving it to the neighbour on the other side of that edge in the closest container laid out in that direction, and \fBwindow \-\-ratio\fP sets the share of the window in its container. Dragging with \fBmouse_action2\fP \fIresize\fP does the same, and with \fBmouse_drag_split\fP \fIon\fP a plain drag on the gap between two tiled windows does too. \fBwindow \-\-toggle split\fP toggles between \fIsplith\fP and \fIsplitv\fP, \fBwindow \-\-toggle zoom\-fullscreen\fP and \fBzoom\-parent\fP make the window cover the space or its container, and \fBspace \-\-balance\fP, \fB\-\-rotate\fP and \fB\-\-mirror\fP work on the whole tree.
.br
In \fBquery \-\-windows\fP, \fIsplit\-type\fP is the layout of the container of the window (\fIvertical\fP for \fIsplith\fP, \fIhorizontal\fP for \fIsplitv\fP, and \fInone\fP for tabbed), \fIstack\-index\fP is the position of its tab in the closest tabbed container
.br
Dropping a window dragged with the mouse onto the edge of another window places it on that side of the window, and dropping it on the center swaps the windows, or makes it a tab of the other window with \fBmouse_drop_action\fP \fIstack\fP. \fBmouse_drop_preview\fP \fIghost\fP shows a translucent copy of the dragged window where it would land while the drag is in progress.
.sp
When the focused window closes, the window focused before it in the same container gets focus, so closing a tab shows the previous tab.
.br
The \fIstack.prev\fP, \fIstack.next\fP, \fIstack.first\fP, \fIstack.last\fP, \fIstack.recent\fP and \fIstack.<index>\fP selectors pick tabs of the closest tabbed container, and \fIprev\fP, \fInext\fP, \fIfirst\fP and \fIlast\fP follow the order of the tree.
.SH "EXIT CODES"
.sp
If \fByabai\fP can\(cqt handle a message, it will return a non\-zero exit code.
.SH "FILES"
.sp
\f(CR$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/yabai/yabai.toml\fP and \f(CR~/.config/yabai/yabai.toml\fP
.RS 4
The config file of the daemon, read at startup and on each reload: the first of the two that exists, unless the daemon
was started with \fB\-\-config\fP (see CONFIGURATION). \f(CR$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fP counts only when it is set and not empty.
.RE
.sp
\f(CR/private/etc/sudoers.d/yabai\fP
.RS 4
Optional, and written by you: the sudoers entry of the built\-in scripting addition load (see CONFIGURATION, \f(CR[daemon]\fP).
.RE
